IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ga-income-tax`

-- Georgia individual income tax -- worked cases, from the seat of the individual taxpayer.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (HB 463/AP as read with ga-2026-act-465-hb-463-ap.formulas.md, and HB 1000/AP), never copied from
-- the encoding's output. Argument order for `How much Georgia income tax do I owe`: taxable year, delayed years, residence,
-- filing status, federal AGI, federal itemized deductions, dependents, my retirement eligibility, my retirement income, my
-- spouse's retirement eligibility, my spouse's retirement income, I am full-time hourly, my overtime, my spouse is full-time
-- hourly, my spouse's overtime, my cash tips, my spouse's cash tips, other subtractions less additions.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The rate, 48-7-20(a.1)(1): 4.99 percent for 2026; less 0.125 a year from 2027; delayed a year per delay; floor 3.99.
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia income tax rate` 2026 0 EQUALS `answered` 0.0499
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia income tax rate` 2027 0 EQUALS `answered` 0.04865
-- Two of the three December determinations (2027, 2028) found a delay: 2030 has taken 2 steps, not 4.
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia income tax rate` 2030 2 EQUALS `answered` 0.0474
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia income tax rate` 2034 0 EQUALS `answered` 0.0399
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia income tax rate` 2045 0 EQUALS `answered` 0.0399
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia income tax rate` 2025 0 EQUALS `not covered` `taxable years before 2026 are governed by the text before HB 463, which is not deposited`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The standard deduction, 48-7-27(a)(1)(B): $30,000 joint, $15,000 otherwise for 2026.
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia standard deduction` 2026 0 `married filing jointly` EQUALS `answered` 30000
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia standard deduction` 2026 0 `head of household` EQUALS `answered` 15000
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia standard deduction` 2026 0 `married filing separately` EQUALS `answered` 15000
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia standard deduction` 2028 0 `single` EQUALS `answered` 15750
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia standard deduction` 2028 1 `married filing jointly` EQUALS `answered` 30750
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia standard deduction` 2040 0 `married filing jointly` EQUALS `answered` 36000
-- At the taxpayer's election, 48-7-27(a)(1)(A) or (B).
#ASSERT `Which Georgia deduction is better for me` 2026 0 `married filing jointly` 35000 EQUALS `answered` 35000
#ASSERT `Which Georgia deduction is better for me` 2026 0 `married filing jointly` 20000 EQUALS `answered` 30000

-- The dependent exemption, 48-7-26(b): $5,000, then $125 a year to $6,000.
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia dependent exemption` 2026 0 EQUALS `answered` 5000
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia dependent exemption` 2028 0 EQUALS `answered` 5250
#ASSERT `What is my Georgia dependent exemption` 2050 3 EQUALS `answered` 6000

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Retirement income, (xiii) to 2026, (xiv) from 2027.
#ASSERT `How much of my retirement income is excluded` 2026 `meets division (iii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 70000 EQUALS `answered` 65000
#ASSERT `How much of my retirement income is excluded` 2027 `meets division (iii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 80000 EQUALS `answered` 70000
#ASSERT `How much of my retirement income is excluded` 2027 `meets division (i) or (ii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 40000 EQUALS `answered` 35000
#ASSERT `How much of my retirement income is excluded` 2027 `meets division (i) or (ii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 12000 EQUALS `answered` 12000
#ASSERT `How much of my retirement income is excluded` 2026 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 12000 EQUALS `answered` 0

-- Overtime, (16): full-time hourly only, up to $1,750, 2026 to 2028.
#ASSERT `How much of my overtime is excluded` 2026 TRUE 2500 EQUALS 1750
#ASSERT `How much of my overtime is excluded` 2026 TRUE 900 EQUALS 900
#ASSERT `How much of my overtime is excluded` 2026 FALSE 900 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much of my overtime is excluded` 2029 TRUE 900 EQUALS 0
-- Cash tips, (17): up to $1,750; the paragraph stands repealed on 31 December 2028 ((17)(E)).
#ASSERT `How much of my cash tips is excluded` 2026 3000 EQUALS 1750
#ASSERT `How much of my cash tips is excluded` 2028 400 EQUALS 400
#ASSERT `How much of my cash tips is excluded` 2029 400 EQUALS 0

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What I owe.
-- A single resident, 2026, $60,000 federal AGI: 60,000 - 15,000 = 45,000; x 4.99% = 2,245.50.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `single` 60000 0 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 2245.5
-- A married couple filing jointly, 2026, $100,000, two dependents: 100,000 - 30,000 - 10,000 = 60,000; x 4.99% = 2,994.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 100000 0 2 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 2994
-- The same couple in 2027 with no delay: 100,000 - 30,750 - 10,250 = 59,000; x 4.865% = 2,870.35.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2027 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 100000 0 2 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 2870.35
-- ... and in 2027 when the 1 December 2026 determination found a delay: the 2026 figures again, 2,994.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2027 1 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 100000 0 2 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 2994
-- ... and in 2034 with no delay, all eight steps taken: 100,000 - 36,000 - 12,000 = 52,000; x 3.99% = 2,074.80.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2034 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 100000 0 2 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 2074.8
-- ... nothing moves after the final figures: 2040 is the same.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2040 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 100000 0 2 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 2074.8
-- A single retiree meeting division (iii), 2026: $80,000 AGI of which $70,000 retirement income: 80,000 - 15,000 - 65,000 = 0.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `single` 80000 0 0 `meets division (iii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 70000 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 0
-- A single person meeting division (i) or (ii), 2026: $60,000 with $40,000 retirement income: 60,000 - 15,000 - 35,000 = 10,000; 499.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `single` 60000 0 0 `meets division (i) or (ii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 40000 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 499
-- A retired couple, 2027, both division (iii), $160,000 with $80,000 and $60,000 retirement income: each spouse's own cap,
-- 70,000 + 60,000; 160,000 - 30,750 - 130,000 = -750: no tax.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2027 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 160000 0 0 `meets division (iii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 80000 `meets division (iii) of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 60000 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 0
-- A single server paid hourly, full time, 2026: $40,000 with $1,000 overtime and $3,000 cash tips:
-- 40,000 - 15,000 - 1,000 - 1,750 = 22,250; x 4.99% = 1,110.275.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `single` 40000 0 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 TRUE 1000 FALSE 0 3000 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 1110.275
-- The same facts in 2029, no delays (3 steps): overtime and tips no longer subtracted; 40,000 - 16,125 = 23,875; x 4.615% = 1,101.83125.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2029 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `single` 40000 0 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 TRUE 1000 FALSE 0 3000 0 0 EQUALS `answered` 1101.83125
-- A joint return in 2026 where both spouses are full-time hourly with overtime and tips: 90,000 - 30,000 - (1,750 + 1,750)
-- overtime - (500 + 1,750) tips = 54,250; x 4.99% = 2,707.075.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `married filing jointly` 90000 0 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 TRUE 2000 TRUE 1750 500 2500 0 EQUALS `answered` 2707.075
-- Not covered: a part-year resident, and a 2025 return.
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2026 0 `part-year resident` `single` 60000 0 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `not covered` `a nonresident or part-year resident's Georgia taxable net income is prorated under Code Section 48-7-85, which is not deposited`
#ASSERT `How much Georgia income tax do I owe` 2025 0 `resident for the whole taxable year` `single` 60000 0 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 `meets no division of 48-7-27(a)(5)(D)` 0 FALSE 0 FALSE 0 0 0 0 EQUALS `not covered` `taxable years before 2026 are governed by the text before HB 463, which is not deposited`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The one-time refund, 48-7-20.4.
#ASSERT `Am I a qualified taxpayer for the one-time refund` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `Am I a qualified taxpayer for the one-time refund` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I a qualified taxpayer for the one-time refund` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS FALSE
-- A student claimed as a dependent for 2024: out without earned income, in with it ((a)(1)(B), (a)(2)).
#ASSERT `Am I a qualified taxpayer for the one-time refund` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `Am I a qualified taxpayer for the one-time refund` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- The lesser of the 2024 liability and $250 / $375 / $500.
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` TRUE 180 `single` FALSE 1 EQUALS 180
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` TRUE 900 `married filing separately` FALSE 1 EQUALS 250
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` TRUE 1000 `head of household` FALSE 1 EQUALS 375
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` TRUE 2000 `married filing jointly` FALSE 1 EQUALS 500
-- Prorated for a part-year resident ((b)(2)): half of $500 is $250; half of the lesser $100 is $50.
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` TRUE 2000 `married filing jointly` TRUE 0.5 EQUALS 250
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` TRUE 100 `married filing jointly` TRUE 0.5 EQUALS 50
#ASSERT `How much is my one-time refund` FALSE 2000 `married filing jointly` FALSE 1 EQUALS 0

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The regulative rules. A breached or still-pending DEONTIC cannot be asserted on the stable l4, so those are #TRACEd with
-- an "-- expect:" line.
-- I file my 2025 return on day 10 of 30; the Department credits $500 and pays it: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`48-7-20.4(a), (b)(1), (d) -- once I file my 2025 return in time, the Department must credit and pay my one-time refund` 30 500) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the taxpayer` `file my 2025 Georgia income tax return` 10), (EVENT `the Department of Revenue` (`credit me with the one-time refund` 500) 40), (EVENT `the Department of Revenue` `pay the refund less any outstanding liability, electronically or by check as my 2025 return instructs` 50)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- I file late (day 40 of 30): no breach by anyone, and nothing is owed to me.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`48-7-20.4(a), (b)(1), (d) -- once I file my 2025 return in time, the Department must credit and pay my one-time refund` 30 500) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the taxpayer` `file my 2025 Georgia income tax return` 40)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: the Department's duty to credit $500 is still open; a credit of $499 does not discharge it.
#TRACE `48-7-20.4(a), (b)(1), (d) -- once I file my 2025 return in time, the Department must credit and pay my one-time refund` 30 500 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the taxpayer` DOES `file my 2025 Georgia income tax return` AT 10
    PARTY `the Department of Revenue` DOES `credit me with the one-time refund` 499 AT 40

-- My employer reports the overtime and tips data on day 25, before the withholding return falls due on day 30: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`48-7-27(a)(16)(C), (17)(B) -- my employer must report overtime and tips with each withholding return` 30) 0 (LIST (EVENT `my employer` `report the overtime and cash tips data for the period to the department` 25)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH by my employer (no report by day 30).
#TRACE `48-7-27(a)(16)(C), (17)(B) -- my employer must report overtime and tips with each withholding return` 30 AT 0 WITH
    (WAITUNTIL 31)
